%[text] # Matrices and Vectors
%[text] This set of exercises reviews the basics of creating, manipulating and doing arithmetic matrices in MATLAB.  The exercises roughly following the presentation in [Introduction to Applied Linear Algebra – Vectors, Matrices, and Least Squares](https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/vmls/), Chapter 6.
%[text] Each of these exercises is intended to be short.  Most can be solved in 1-2 lines of code.  None of them should take more than 4 lines of code.
%[text] ## Creating and Indexing Matrices
%[text] **Create a matrix** 
%[text] ![](text:image:17e8)
% Your code here.       
%[text] Use `size` and `fprintf` functions to print the number of rows and columns in A.  For example
%[text] ```
%[text] A has m rows and n columns.
%[text] ```
%[text] where `m` and `n` are integer values.
% Your code here.       
%[text] **Indexing and slicing**
%[text] Use matrix indexing to extract the following from A:
%[text] - The element in the 2nd row and 3rd column
%[text] - The second row
%[text] - The last column
%[text] - The submatrix ![](text:image:7c87) \
% Your code here.       
%[text] **Block matrices**
%[text] Create these four matrices
%[text] ![](text:image:11bc)
% Your code here.       
%[text] Then use those submatrices to concatenate and stack into this new matrix.
%[text] ![](text:image:3395)
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] % Horizontal concatenation (side by side)
%[text] C = [A B]       % or use horzcat(A,B)
%[text] % Vertical concatenation (one above the other)
%[text] C = [A; B]      % or use vertcat(A,B)
%[text] ```
%[text] Hint: It might be easier to debug to do this one step at a time, incrementally building A from the parts.
% Your code here.       
%[text] **Column representation of a matrix.**
%[text] Create threse three 3x1 matrices (vectors) using the commands `zeros`, `ones` and using scalar matrix multiplcation.
%[text] ![](text:image:511c) ![](text:image:6e70) ![](text:image:372f) ![](text:image:4610)
% Your code here.       
%[text] Create a 3x4 matrix from these three columns, where each vector is column.
% Your code here.       
%[text] Create a 4x3 matrix from these three vectors, where each vector is row without transposing the entire matrix C. (Hint: you can transpose each vector.)
% Your code here.       
%[text] Diagonal matrices
%[text] Use the `diag` function to create this matrix.
%[text] ![](text:image:3bb3)
% Your code here.       
%[text] **Transpose of block matrix, matrix equivalence and** **`all`**
%[text] Create two matrices 2x2 matrices
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] A = reshape(1:4,[2,2])
%[text] B = reshape(11:14, [2, 2])
%[text] ```
% Your code here.       
%[text] Show that $\\left\[A \\, \\, B\\right\]^T = \\left\[ \\matrix{A^T \\cr B^T}\\right\] $.   To show that two matrices, M and N,  are equivalent use **`all( M == N, "all")`**`.`
% Your code here.       
%[text] **Transponse of sum**
%[text] Use the same A and B matrices to show that
%[text] $(A+B)^T = A^T + B^T$
% Your code here.       
%[text] **Scalar-matrix multiplication**
%[text] Create a 3x2 matrix of uniformly distributed random integers using the **`randi`** function. (You choose the `imax` parameter.)
% Your code here.       
%[text] Show that $( \\beta + \\gamma) A = \\beta A+ \\gamma A$
%[text] where $\\beta\n$ and $\\gamma$ are integer (numeric doubles with integer values) scalars of your choosing. 
% Your code here.       
%[text] Does te same equivalence hold if A is a uniformaly distributed decimal number from **`rand`** function? What about if the scalars are non-integers?
%[text] **Matrix-vector multiplication**
%[text] Create a matrix and a vector to show that this simple example is true
%[text] ![](text:image:26e1)
% Your code here.       
